(PREFACE.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Some years ago I came across a book, called ?Crump?s Theory of Stock) Tj T*
(Speculation,? which had gone through several editions in England; and the) Tj T*
(practical wisdom expressed therein impressed me so forcibly that ever) Tj T*
(since then I formed the project of publishing an American edition.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The reader may be astonished that, as a broker, I desire to give such a) Tj T*
(book a larger circulation than it possessed heretofore, as the natural) Tj T*
(conclusion would be that it might injure my business. I feel, however,) Tj T*
(that it is not so, and think that a broker loses nothing by doing his) Tj T*
(duty in warning his clients against danger, and showing them the pitfalls.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(I do not quite agree with the author of the book on every point,) Tj T*
(especially when he seeks to convey the impression that it would seem) Tj T*
(almost impossible that any profit could be derived from Stock Exchange) Tj T*
(speculation. During my long experience I have seen many speculators) Tj T*
(accumulate large fortunes, and I believe that when speculation is) Tj T*
(conducted by a clear-headed man, as a matter of business and not as a) Tj T*
(matter of amusement, it offers great chances.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Where the strength of Mr. Crump?s work lies is in his showing what) Tj T*
(attributes of character a man must possess to be successful, and with) Tj T*
(these attributes a man must prove successful in stock speculation as well) Tj T*
(as in any other business. Condensed, these attributes are: first, a clear) Tj T*
(head; second, capital; and third, patience.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(I do not agree with Mr. Crump, that a speculator, to be successful,) Tj T*
ET
endstream
endobj
